From 19e690ad36e84cc9a1b615ce7f2aaf614be7e7fa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yekai pan <pyk@seas.upenn.edu>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 23:31:18 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 13/17] [trmc] keep a self call in place across a release with
 an observable cleanup

TRMC re-creates a linearized self call at its leaf and so moves it past
the releases between the two, which it took for bookkeeping. A release
that runs a foreign cleanup hook, or drops a closure that may have
captured a foreign object, is observable: the hook then ran before the
recursion instead of after it, so a handle that the source keeps open
during the recursive call was closed first.

Such a release now keeps the call in place and its leaf takes the apply
fallback. Inside an expanded decrement every operation that is neither
read-only nor count bookkeeping is tested by the types of its operands:
the `rc.dec` of the foreign object, a call of drop glue, a drop callback
such as `drop.run`. A record that reports an observable cleanup is asked
member by member, and an arbitrary-precision integer is not observable,
so a release that only reaches integers does not pin the call.

On the benchmark suite, core and std (33 units, -Oaggressive
--reuse-across-call) all 18 `.trmc` helpers and 114 calls of a helper
are kept and the lowered MLIR is byte-identical.
